---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// Preserve the existing URL. The human-focused route is under discussion.
const project = projects.thesis;
---

<Base
  title={`${project.title} · Xiaochuan Li`}
  description="Master’s thesis under discussion: how epistemic expressions affect later access to a specific possibility. An adult behavioural study is proposed; the final focus and computational scope remain open."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Current Work</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">{project.status}</div>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">{project.question}</p>
    <p>{project.summary} {project.connection}</p>

    <h2>The connection to the formal analysis</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/research/three-ways">Three Ways of Leaving <em>p</em> Unsettled</a> examines what
      possibility claims, explicit bilateral openness, and ignorance reports contribute to discourse.
      The thesis proposal asks an empirical question about the consequences of such differences:
      whether an expression changes how readily a listener accesses a particular possibility later on.
      The formal analysis motivates the comparison; it does not establish a psychological effect.
    </p>

    <h2>A proposed adult behavioural study</h2>
    <p>
      One candidate design compares expressions such as “It might be a lemon” and “I don’t know
      whether it is a lemon” in matched contexts. A speeded detection or verification task could
      measure subsequent access to the target, with related and unrelated probes and controls for
      lexical mention, delay, and later evidence excluding the possibility.
    </p>
    <p>
      The intended test is whether a content-specific difference survives those controls and changes
      appropriately when new evidence arrives. Faster responses alone would not establish a new
      semantic representation. The materials, task, analysis, and feasible scope need to be agreed
      before data collection. A well-supported absence of the predicted effect would also constrain
      the proposed account.
    </p>

    <h2>The computational question</h2>
    <p>
      Earlier versions of the proposal prioritised controlled language-model training: which input
      relations could enable a learner to acquire and generalise a specified discourse behaviour?
      That remains a research interest. The current discussion concerns which question should be
      the thesis’s primary contribution and whether a limited model comparison would serve it.
      A full human experiment and a full training study are not jointly committed deliverables.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      The thesis is in design. A human-focused direction is being considered; its primary empirical
      focus has not yet been finalised. No adult data have been collected. Preliminary
      model-scoring checks have tested the measurement pipeline and exposed material confounds;
      they have not established the target semantic effect. There are no confirmatory thesis results.
    </p>
    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]"><a href="/research">← Back to Current Work</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .cw-status { font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text); padding: 1rem 1.2rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); background: var(--color-surface); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; }
</style>
